    color: var(--gray);
    text-align: center;
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
  <header>
    <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023)</p>
    <p class="meta">Source: UNODC Crime Trends Survey (CTS) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Policy Analysts &amp; Public Safety Stakeholders &nbsp;|&nbsp; Generated: 2026-04-29</p>
  </header>

  <section id="executive-summary">
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <p>
      This report examines three decades of intentional homicide data from the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) to answer a central question: <strong>Is the world becoming safer from intentional homicide, and for whom?</strong> The evidence points to a nuanced picture. While the global <em>median</em> homicide rate has declined from roughly 3.0 per 100,000 in the mid-1990s to <strong>{stats['global_median_recent']:.2f}</strong> in {stats['most_recent_year']}, the global <em>mean</em> remains elevated at <strong>{stats['global_mean_recent']:.2f}</strong>, driven by a small number of extremely high-rate countries. Decade-over-decade analysis reveals a modest improvement from the 2000s to the 2010s (−9.6%), but the 2020s have seen a slight rebound (+0.2%).
    </p>
    <p>
      Gender disparity remains stark and persistent: males are homicide victims at a median rate roughly <strong>{stats['recent_mf_median']:.1f}×</strong> that of females, a ratio that has changed little over the observation period. Young adults—particularly males aged 18–29—face the highest risk. Meanwhile, several Caribbean and Latin American nations continue to report alarming rates, while a number of Asian and European countries have achieved dramatic long-term reductions.
    </p>
    <div class="callout warning">
      <strong>Data Caveat:</strong> This is an unbalanced panel. Only <strong>{stats['coverage_recent']:.1f}%</strong> of the {stats['total_countries']} countries in the dataset reported data for {stats['most_recent_year']}. Coverage peaked in 2019 at 137 countries but has since fallen. Comparisons across time and geography must be interpreted with this limitation in mind.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="kpi-dashboard">
    <h2>Key Performance Indicators</h2>
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <span class="kpi-value">{stats['global_median_recent']:.2f}</span>
        <span class="kpi-label">Global Median Homicide Rate ({stats['most_recent_year']})<br><small>per 100,000 population</small></span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card secondary">
        <span class="kpi-value">{stats['recent_mf_median']:.1f}×</span>
        <span class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Homicide Rate Ratio ({stats['most_recent_year']})<br><small>median across reporting countries</small></span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <span class="kpi-value">{stats['highest_national_rate']:.1f}</span>
        <span class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate ({stats['most_recent_year']})<br><small>{stats['highest_country']}</small></span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card teal">
        <span class="kpi-value">−9.6%</span>
        <span class="kpi-label">Decade Change (2000s → 2010s)<br><small>global average rate</small></span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <span class="kpi-value">{stats['coverage_recent']:.1f}%</span>
        <span class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage ({stats['most_recent_year']})<br><small>of {stats['total_countries']} countries</small></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="global-overview">
    <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The global trajectory of intentional homicide is best understood by distinguishing between the median and mean. The <strong>median</strong>—the rate of the typical reporting country—has trended downward since the mid-1990s, suggesting that the "average" nation has become safer. However, the <strong>mean</strong> is heavily skewed by outliers, particularly in the Caribbean and Central America, and has fluctuated between 6.8 and 8.5 over the past decade.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_global_trend']}
    </div>
    <p>
      The dotted grey line overlays the number of countries reporting each year, highlighting a critical data quality issue. Coverage expanded steadily from the early 1990s, peaked at 137 countries in 2019, but dropped sharply during the COVID-19 era to just 84 countries by 2023. This decline means recent global aggregates may not be directly comparable to earlier years: the composition of reporting nations has shifted, and some high-rate countries may be underrepresented in the latest figures.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_decades']}
    </div>
    <p>
      Decade-level aggregation smooths out year-to-year volatility. The 1990s and 2000s were comparably violent on average (8.27 and 8.51 respectively). The 2010s registered a meaningful decline to 7.70, but the 2020s—to date—have seen a marginal uptick to 7.71. Whether this represents a genuine reversal or a reporting-artifact remains uncertain given the shrinking panel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="regional-comparison">
    <h2>2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>
    <p>
      Geographic disparities are extreme. In the most recent year with data, the highest national rate—<strong>{stats['highest_national_rate']:.1f}</strong> per 100,000 in {stats['highest_country']}—is orders of magnitude above the safest nations. The following tables rank the ten highest and ten lowest rates among countries reporting in {stats['most_recent_year']}.
    </p>
    <h3>Top 10 Highest Rates ({stats['most_recent_year']})</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_top10']}
    </div>
    <p>
      The concentration of high-rate countries in the Caribbean (Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, Jamaica, Saint Lucia, Trinidad and Tobago) and Latin America (Ecuador, Honduras, Colombia, Mexico, Costa Rica) is unmistakable. These small-island and middle-income states face compounded risk factors including gang violence, drug trafficking corridors, and strained criminal justice capacity.
    </p>
    <h3>Bottom 10 Lowest Rates ({stats['most_recent_year']})</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Rate (per 100,000)</th><th>Context</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Holy See</td><td>0.00</td><td>Very small population; zero reported homicides</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Singapore</td><td>0.07</td><td>Long-standing low-rate outlier in Southeast Asia</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Oman</td><td>0.14</td><td>Consistently low since early 2000s</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Japan</td><td>0.23</td><td>Stable, ultra-low rate for decades</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>Hong Kong</td><td>0.38</td><td>Dramatic decline from 2.46 in 1990</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>South Korea</td><td>0.48</td><td>Steady downward trend since 1990s</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7</td><td>Macao</td><td>0.56</td><td>Declined from 3.31 in 1991</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>Malta</td><td>0.56</td><td>Consistently low Mediterranean rate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9</td><td>Slovenia</td><td>0.57</td><td>Among Europe's safest nations</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>Italy</td><td>0.57</td><td>Down from 3.15 in 1990</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Insight:</strong> The gap between the highest and lowest national rates exceeds <strong>64 points</strong> per 100,000—more than two orders of magnitude. This disparity underscores that "global" averages can mask radically different local realities.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sex-disparity">
    <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
    <p>
      One of the most consistent findings across the dataset is the profound gender imbalance in homicide victimization. Males are killed at a median rate roughly three times that of females, and in many countries the ratio is far higher. In {stats['most_recent_year']}, the median male homicide rate among reporting countries was <strong>{stats['male_median_recent']:.2f}</strong> per 100,000, compared with <strong>{stats['female_median_recent']:.2f}</strong> for females.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_mf_ratio']}
    </div>
    <p>
      The male-to-female ratio has remained remarkably stable over three decades, fluctuating between approximately 2.9 and 3.5 at the median. This persistence suggests that the structural drivers of male victimization—gang involvement, firearm violence, and street-level conflict—are deeply entrenched and resistant to broad secular trends. The mean ratio is higher (around 4.3–4.7) because a subset of countries exhibits extreme skew, driven by very high male rates in conflict or gang-affected settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is important to note that while males dominate aggregate victim counts, females may face distinct risk profiles—particularly intimate-partner and family-related homicide—that are not fully captured by overall rate comparisons. The UNODC data do not disaggregate relationship to perpetrator, so this analysis is limited to sex-disaggregated rates only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="age-group-analysis">
    <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>
    <p>
      Age-disaggregated data reveal that homicide is disproportionately a killer of young adults. The 25–29 age group records the highest mean rate globally (16.6 per 100,000), followed closely by 20–24 (14.9) and 18–19 (12.1). Children under 10 and adolescents 10–14 face the lowest risk, although any non-zero rate in these groups is especially concerning from a child-protection perspective.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_age_profile']}
    </div>
    <p>
      The risk profile is not uniform across sex. When disaggregated, males aged 18–29 show median rates an order of magnitude higher than their female peers. The following chart compares median rates by age group and sex on a logarithmic scale to accommodate the wide range.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_age_sex']}
    </div>
    <p>
      For males, the peak risk window is 25–29 (median 4.0), with elevated rates extending from 18–19 through 30–44. For females, risk is more evenly distributed across age groups, with a modest peak in the 25–29 and 30–44 brackets (median ~1.1–1.4). Elderly populations (60+) face moderate risk relative to youth, but again with a strong male skew.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Policy implication:</strong> Violence-prevention programs targeting males aged 15–29—through education, employment, and gang-disengagement interventions—are likely to yield the largest reductions in overall homicide burden.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="notable-trends">
    <h2>5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>
    <p>
      Beyond snapshot comparisons, longitudinal analysis reveals dramatic divergences in national trajectories. The following charts highlight countries with the largest percentage changes between their earliest and latest available observations over a span of at least ten years.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {svgs['svg_changes']}
    </div>
    <h3>Remarkable Success Stories</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>El Salvador</strong> stands out as the most dramatic turnaround: from a peak of 134.3 per 100,000 in 1994 to 7.9 in 2022—a decline of 94%. <strong>Singapore</strong> and <strong>Hong Kong</strong> both reduced already-low rates by over 80%, cementing their positions among the world's safest jurisdictions. <strong>Italy</strong> and <strong>Macao</strong> also achieved reductions exceeding 80% from 1990s baselines. These cases demonstrate that steep declines are possible across diverse institutional and cultural contexts.
    </p>
    <h3>Alarming Deteriorations</h3>
    <p>
      On the other end of the spectrum, <strong>Haiti</strong> saw its rate surge from 5.2 in 2007 to 41.2 in 2023—a nearly sevenfold increase amid political instability and gang proliferation. <strong>Saint Kitts and Nevis</strong> climbed from 9.5 in 1995 to 64.2 in 2023, while <strong>Ecuador</strong> and <strong>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</strong> both more than quintupled their rates. These deteriorations are concentrated in the Caribbean and parts of Latin America, reinforcing the region's status as the global epicenter of lethal violence.
    </p>
    <h3>Volatility</h3>
    <p>
      Several small states and territories exhibit extreme year-to-year volatility, driven by low population denominators and stochastic event counts. San Marino, Saint Helena, and Tuvalu show the highest coefficients of variation. For these jurisdictions, single-year figures should be interpreted with caution; multi-year averages are more reliable indicators of underlying risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
    <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <p>
      This analysis is subject to important constraints that affect interpretation:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report every year. The set of reporting nations changes over time, which can introduce compositional bias into global and regional averages.</li>
      <li><strong>Coverage decline:</strong> Reporting coverage fell from a peak of 137 countries (68% of the panel) in 2019 to just 84 (42%) in 2023. The reasons for this decline—whether delayed statistical processing, resource constraints, or political factors—are unknown from the data alone.</li>
      <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data refer to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately where available and should not be double-counted.</li>
      <li><strong>Territories:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are listed separately. Analysts should treat these as distinct reporting units.</li>
      <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years are omitted, not interpolated. Trend estimates for individual countries with sparse data should be treated as indicative rather than definitive.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
    <p>
      Based on the evidence presented, the following recommendations are offered for policy analysts and public safety stakeholders:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Target youth and gender-specific interventions.</strong> Given the concentration of risk among males aged 15–29, gender-transformative programming, mentorship, and economic inclusion initiatives should be prioritized in high-rate settings.</li>
      <li><strong>Invest in Caribbean and Latin American capacity.</strong> The clustering of extreme rates and rapid deteriorations in this region demands coordinated international assistance for law enforcement, judicial reform, and violence-prevention ecosystems.</li>
      <li><strong>Learn from rapid decliners.</strong> Case studies of El Salvador, Singapore, Hong Kong, and Italy should be systematically evaluated to identify transferable policies—whether community policing, firearm regulation, or social investment—that contributed to their success.</li>
      <li><strong>Strengthen data infrastructure.</strong> The decline in global reporting coverage is itself a risk factor for effective policy. Donors and international organizations should support national statistical and criminal-justice data systems, particularly in fragile states.</li>
      <li><strong>Disaggregate further.</strong> Future data collection should prioritize relationship-to-perpetrator and mechanism (firearm, sharp object, etc.) variables to enable more targeted prevention strategies, particularly for female victimization.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> The world is not uniformly becoming safer. While many nations have achieved remarkable reductions, others—especially in the Caribbean and parts of Latin America—are experiencing severe deteriorations. Gender and age disparities remain entrenched. Closing these gaps requires targeted, evidence-based interventions and a renewed commitment to transparent, timely data.
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p>Report generated from UNODC Crime Trends Survey data (1990–2023) via Kaggle dataset <em>lucalullo/global-homicide-rates-by-country</em>.</p>
    <p>All statistics derived directly from the provided dataset. No external sources or imputed values were used.</p>
  </footer>
</div>
</body>
</html>
'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("Report written to /app/output/final_report.html")
PYEOF
